  <h1 id="top">Low Sodium Beef Jerky: The Complete Guide (Brands, Sodium Facts & a Recipe)</h1>

  <div class="answer-box">
    <p><strong>Low sodium beef jerky</strong> is jerky with under 200 mg of sodium per one-ounce serving — roughly half the sodium of a standard jerky bag. Brands like Country Archer, Jerky Gent, and Strollo's meet this bar; you can also make it at home with a dry-brine technique that skips the soy sauce and cuts salt in half.</p>
  </div>

  <ContentImage src="/images/low-sodium-beef-jerky-hero.webp" alt="A pile of dark low sodium beef jerky strips on a wooden board next to a nutrition label showing under 200 mg of sodium per serving" eager={true} />

  <p>I have spent fifteen years salting meat for a living, and here is the thing nobody in the jerky business likes to say out loud: <strong>most beef jerky is aggressively salty</strong>. A single 3-ounce bag can hit 60% of your entire daily sodium budget, and it takes about ninety seconds to eat. <em>[Switches to serious face]</em> That is a real problem for anyone watching blood pressure, on a DASH diet, or just trying to eat cleaner. The good news: low sodium beef jerky is a genuine category now, both from smart brands and from a home dry-brine method that took me a few batches to nail down. This is the full guide — the actual numbers, the honest brand comparison, and the recipe.</p>

