  <h2 id="how-much-sodium-in-beef-jerky">How Much Sodium Is in Beef Jerky</h2>
  <p><strong>Regular beef jerky contains 400 to 700 milligrams of sodium per one-ounce serving</strong>, with most mass-market brands landing squarely in the middle of that range. To put that in perspective: the <a href="https://www.heart.org/en/healthy-living/healthy-eating/eat-smart/sodium/how-much-sodium-should-i-eat-per-day" rel="noopener" target="_blank">American Heart Association</a> recommends adults cap daily sodium at 2,300 mg — and ideally 1,500 mg for anyone with high blood pressure. A typical 3-ounce bag of Jack Link's Original packs about 1,590 mg. That is 69% of your entire daily allowance in one snack.</p>
  <p>Why is jerky this salty in the first place? Two reasons, both structural to how jerky is made:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Salt is a preservative.</strong> It draws moisture out of the meat, which is exactly what dehydration does, and inhibits bacteria growth during the long low-temperature dry. Historically salt was the whole preservation strategy.</li>
    <li><strong>Wet marinades use soy sauce and Worcestershire as the flavor base.</strong> A tablespoon of regular soy sauce alone is 900 mg of sodium. Even "low-sodium" soy sauce runs 550 mg per tablespoon. Multiply by the volume in a marinade and you see how quickly the numbers stack.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>The USDA's <a href="https://fdc.nal.usda.gov/food-details/174510/nutrients" rel="noopener" target="_blank">FoodData Central</a> average for beef jerky puts it at about 590 mg of sodium per ounce, confirming what the label survey tells you: this is a genuinely high-sodium food unless a brand actively engineers around it, or you make it yourself.</p>

  <ContentImage src="/images/low-sodium-beef-jerky-nutrition-label.webp" alt="A beef jerky nutrition label with the sodium content circled showing a low sodium beef jerky reading of 180 milligrams per serving" />

  <h2 id="is-there-a-low-sodium-beef-jerky">Is There a Low Sodium Beef Jerky</h2>
  <p>Short answer: yes — <strong>low sodium beef jerky is a real category</strong>, and it has grown considerably in the last five years as consumer demand for lower-sodium snacks caught up with the jerky boom. The practical threshold most brands and reviewers use is <strong>under 200 mg of sodium per one-ounce serving</strong>, roughly half a normal bag.</p>
  <p>The FDA reserves the strict "low sodium" label for foods with 140 mg or less per serving, and a small number of jerky brands hit that mark specifically to earn the on-package claim. Anything from 140 to 300 mg is meaningfully lower than typical jerky and worth the swap if you are actively watching salt intake. Above 300 mg the marketing gets creative but the sodium reality does not change much.</p>
  <p>Where to find it: specialty jerky sites (jerkygent.com, savagejerky.com), Whole Foods, Sprouts, and increasingly the meat-snack aisle at Kroger and Walmart. Amazon is the reliable one-stop for bulk. See the brand comparison in the next section for actual sodium numbers.</p>

