  <p>The pattern: <strong>anything under 200 mg per ounce is genuinely low sodium</strong>. Strollo's and People's Choice are the two that clear the strict FDA threshold. Country Archer is the middle-ground pick you can find in a normal grocery store. Jack Link's is included at the bottom purely so you can see the delta — a factor of six between the lowest and the most common.</p>
  <p>Nutrition numbers change; brands reformulate; check the current label before you commit. But the ranking has been stable for a couple of years.</p>

  <ContentImage src="/images/low-sodium-beef-jerky-brands.webp" alt="A lineup of low sodium beef jerky bags including Country Archer, Chomps, and other brands arranged side by side for comparison" />

  <h2 id="how-to-make-low-sodium-beef-jerky">How to Make Low Sodium Beef Jerky</h2>
  <p>The trick to <strong>how to make low sodium beef jerky</strong> at home is one word: <strong>dry-brine</strong>. Every wet-marinade jerky recipe you find online leans hard on soy sauce for its flavor base, and that is where the sodium spirals. A dry-brine uses a fraction of the salt to deliver most of the flavor, because it draws moisture out of the meat, dissolves the seasoning, and reabsorbs it as a concentrated crust — no soy-sauce sodium bomb required.</p>
  <p>This is my go-to recipe, adapted from years of testing every "low salt" variation I could think of. It comes in around 180 mg of sodium per finished ounce — cleaner than every commercial brand except Strollo's:</p>

