  <p>Two technique notes that matter more than they look:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Dry-brine beats wet marinade for sodium control every time.</strong> A typical wet marinade with 1/3 cup soy sauce delivers ~3,000 mg of sodium into a 2 lb batch (about 190 mg per finished ounce from soy alone). My dry-brine version above uses 1 teaspoon of kosher salt for the same batch — that's about 2,300 mg total, spread across ~12 oz of finished jerky, so roughly 190 mg per ounce total. The dry-brine gets you the same flavor at a fraction of the added salt.</li>
    <li><strong>MSG is your ally, not your enemy.</strong> The half-teaspoon of MSG replaces the "savory salty punch" you would normally get from more salt or soy sauce, and it delivers roughly one-third the sodium per gram. Widely-cited research has debunked the old MSG scare stories; the FDA classifies it as generally recognized as safe.</li>
  </ul>
